# Test droppings from tests/test_fitpl.py (created in cwd, not cleaned up)
test_powerlaw*.db

# figure-generation panel cache
Manuscript/figures/_panel_*.parquet

# run_ablation.py optional panel cache. The ablation *outputs*
# (ablation_results.csv, ablation_city.csv and their .meta.json) are tracked:
# snapshot_manifest.json hashes them, so a regeneration must show up in
# `git status` instead of being hidden by an ignore rule.
_ablation_panel*.parquet

# Vendored JS deps and slide build output (restored by `npm ci` / `node compile.js`)
node_modules/
slides/output/

# LaTeX structural aux (the PDFs stay tracked on purpose)
*.fdb_latexmk
*.fls
*.nav
*.snm
*.vrb

# chain run logs
logs/
powerlaw_results.db.bak*

# fig01 (aggregation schematic) was dropped by PR #10 and must stay deleted
Manuscript/figures/fig01_aggregation_levels.*

# fig02 (box plot of alpha per construction) was dropped: the same distributions
# are drawn, by construction and over common bins, in fig07_alpha_by_construction,
# which also carries the three size constructions. the summary table (tab:main) answers
# the same question numerically, so the box plot was reporting a third time.
Manuscript/figures/fig02_alpha_by_level.*

# The two-panel version of fig07, superseded by the four-panel
# fig07_alpha_by_construction. Ignored so a stale copy cannot be committed back.
Manuscript/figures/fig07_window_flip.*

# fig08 (stability of the two exponents) was dropped: panel (a) restated the
# spread of alpha_S that the text already gives beside its sampling noise, and
# panel (b) plotted the alpha_W/population correlation whose three numbers the
# text states in full and make_tables.py audits from the database. Nothing in the
# figure was a fact the prose did not already carry, so it is not regenerated.
Manuscript/figures/fig08_alpha_stability.*

# fig16 and fig17 (the survival reading) were dropped: the Kaplan-Meier panel
# repeated fig09 on a pooled sample, and the AIC bar chart restated numbers the
# text gives. survival_analysis.py still writes both to Manuscript/figures when
# it is run -- they are a diagnostic, so they are ignored rather than deleted
# from the script, and a stale copy cannot be committed back.
Manuscript/figures/fig16_survival_exceedance.*
Manuscript/figures/fig17_survival_duration.*

# survival_analysis.py tables and summary: regenerated, and committed anyway
# because snapshot_manifest.json hashes them.
analysis/
